    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 071941\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1241 PM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Elevated flows will continue on the Salt and Gila rivers \n  through at least early next week due to dam releases. \n\n- Dry weather with occasional breeziness and temperatures near to\n  slightly above the daily normals will be common through much of\n  next week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nCurrent 500 mb RAP analysis places the center of an upper low \nover Sonora early this afternoon, with notable height packing \nalong its western periphery, resulting in a northerly jet streak \nstretching from over the Lower CO River southward over the \nnorthern Baja. To the north of this upper low, midlevel ridging is\napparent stretching from off the CA Coast inland over the Great \nBasin, allowing positive H5 height anomalies to nudge into the \nforecast area this weekend as the upper low departs to the south. \nHigher momentum air began to mix down to the surface this morning \nfor portions of the western deserts, along the Lower CO River \nValley, resulting in breezy north winds with gusts upwards of \n20-30 mph observed and locally as much as 35 mph. Further east \nacross South-Central AZ, winds are generally lighter at the \nsurface and more so driven by a northeasterly SLP gradient which \nhas developed as a result of this pattern, with relatively high \nsurface pressure established over the Colorado Plateau (11.72 mb \ngradient observed between Snowflake and KPHX at 12Z this morning).\nGradients will relax through the rest of the weekend but are \nexpected to persist into early next week such that wind directions\nacross the forecast area continue to favor northeast.\n\nRising midlevel heights and a rather quick response in thermal \nprofiles will result in warmer temperatures over the weekend into \nearly next week. Ensemble mean H5 heights will reach a 581-585 dam\nrange, between the 55th and 80th percentile of climatology for \nthis time of year. Despite the positive midlevel height anomalies \nand warming midlevel thermal profiles, ensemble mean 850-700 mb \ntemperatures will still remain generally in a 30th-60th percentile\nof climatology range (850 mb temperatures between 12C-16C). \nHowever, downslope flow off the Colorado Plateau and some \nadiabatic compression will ensure surface temperatures remain \nseasonably warm, with latest NBM highs today through Monday in the\nlower to middle 80s across the lower deserts (warmer out west), \nbetween 2F-5F above daily normals. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY through FRIDAY/...\nHemispheric pattern amplification driven by an unusually early \nseason sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) event will be the main \nstory for the latter half of next week with large ensemble \nforecast spread persisting. For much of the week, northern stream \njet energy will settle over southern Canada while weak low \namplitude waves in the southern stream remain sequestered over \nnorthern Mexico. As a result, subsident ridging with nearly steady\nmidtropospheric heights will define the pattern over the SW Conus\nyielding high confidence of a very tranquil, near persistence \nforecast carrying on from early in the week. By the end of the \nweek, effects from disruption of the polar vortex will be \nmanifested in poleward retrogressive ridging while deep negative \nheight anomalies descending equatorward over the NE Conus and East\nPacific. However, the specifics regarding depth, positioning, and\nevolution of features within the greater synoptic pattern remain \nhighly uncertain. Recent operational models peel a notable \nshortwave from the east Pacific trough towards the SW \nConus/northern Mexico late in the week, however this type of \nsolution is not consistent among NAEFS and EPS members. Given the \namplification phase, wide ensemble spread and large run-to-run \nvariations are likely the next several days resulting in low \nforecast confidence for next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1710Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo aviation weather concerns under mostly clear skies can be\nexpected throughout the TAF period. Easterly winds will speeds\ngenerally aob 10 kts will prevail throughout the period. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation weather concerns under clear skies can be expected\nthroughout the TAF period. Winds will generally fluctuate out of a\nnorth to northwest direction with speeds generally under 12 kts. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions will prevail across the region through the majority \nof next week with some enhanced morning ridge top and terrain gap \nbreeziness during the beginning of the week. Minimum afternoon \nhumidity levels will primarily fall into a 20-30% range in western \ndistricts and 30-50% in eastern districts with a tendency for drying \ntowards the middle/end of the week. This will follow fair to good \novernight recovery in western districts and good to excellent levels \nin eastern districts. Outside of the early week breeziness, wind \nspeeds will be light with directional tendencies towards a \nnortherly or easterly component. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Whittock\nLONG TERM...18\nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
